unzip
open folder
double click on the 'index.html' file to open the file in your web browser to open as is.

log in using username in the username box, and your uid in the password box.
click login to login.
cancel will close the modal, but does not log anyone in or refresh the page.
login will attempt to log in the user, if successful it will load up the user information they have permission to see
    - users with the 'User' role, (actually with any non-admin non-mod role) have permission to see
        - user information of those with the Admin and Mod roles *Note: unless hidden 
        - thier own user information
    - users with the Admin and Mod roles can see all users
        - they can also hide their user information from all non-admin, non-mod roles by clicking the hide button
            - the button then changes to a show button, which will un-hide the admin/mod 
            - *Note: this resets to visible when the page refreshes or reloads
There is a menu bar with a single button.
    - when someone has closed the login modal this button is labeled 'login', and  will re-open it
    - when someone has successfully logged in this will change to a 'change user' button
        - clicking on 'change user' will log the current user out and open up the login modal
---
Any user changes require editing the files *before* opening:

editing users and admin roles needs to be done in the script.js file
    - change a user's first name, last name, and derived email information by: 
        - calling the 'updateUserName()' function.
            - this should be done in the noted section ( approximately line 85 ) in the file to ensure the changes take immediately
            - this funtion needs, in order (none can be skipped): 
                -user Index Number, first name, last name
                    - example: updateUserName(5, 'Cole', 'Mercer');
                    - the user's Index Number is the position in 'baseUsers' array (note, first position is index 0, second is index 1)
                    - it is also the numver used at the end of the username 'user#'
                        - example: user5 has an index number of 5
    - make a user an admin
        - call the 'makeAdmin()' function
            - this funtion needs, in order (none can be skipped): 
                -user Index Number, new role
                    - the only roles supported by default are 'Admin' 'Mod' and 'User'
                        - these ARE case-sensitive
                        - the admin and mod user types have higher priviledges (detailed later), and have slightly different presentation
                - example: makeAdmin(0, "Admin");
    
